WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:08.790
Math is really beautiful, but many students
don't ever get a chance to see it because

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:15.540
we hide that beauty until way too late in
a math student's educational experience.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:21.690
We're hiding whole amazing areas of mathematics
until the students reach college.

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:23.820
But many of the students don't make it that
far.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:27.170
They decide before they get there that there's
nothing to see here.

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:30.730
They say they're not a math person because
they're bored with math, because they've been

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:32.550
taught that math is arithmetic.

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:35.850
They haven't been taught that math is discovery.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:38.109
Math is a creative discipline.

00:00:38.109 --> 00:00:42.559
I know a lot of artistically creative people,
the most creative people I know that are mathematicians.

00:00:42.559 --> 00:00:47.870
You know, they have to create new frameworks
in which to create new knowledge.They are

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:51.379
creating castles in the sky.

00:00:51.379 --> 00:00:52.379
My name is Richard Rusczyk.

00:00:52.379 --> 00:00:54.460
I'm the founder of Art of Problem Solving.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:59.069
We develop educational resources and opportunities
for students who are really interested in

00:00:59.069 --> 00:01:00.069
learning.

00:01:00.069 --> 00:01:03.420
I did not learn very much in the classroom.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:04.920
We didn't have the Internet then.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.570
So there was no you know, there was no YouTube
to watch videos.

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:11.580
But I learned a lot from textbooks and working
on problems.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:15.650
I had a stack of formula sheets and I would
just memorize them and I would eventually

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:20.920
get to a training program for the students
who did well in a series of national competitions.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:22.350
I'm there for five weeks.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:30.110
I see 50 or 60 problems during this time,
and I got exactly zero correct.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:34.000
I had to go back to those formulas and ask,
why is this true?

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.530
Why is that true?

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:41.240
And realized that I did not know and had to
start trying to prove some of these things.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:47.110
I was coming out of a high school that in
northern Alabama, it was an aggressively average

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:48.110
high school.

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:51.040
But I go off to Princeton and I'm worried
about competing against students that went

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:54.030
to magnet school, private schools.

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:58.480
And I found in the first few weeks it was
very clear that I had it backwards.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:01:59.480
I had training.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:00.990
They didn't have.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:05.660
And that training was all those years staring
at problems I didn't know how to do and having

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:07.500
to learn how to fight through them.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:12.290
I had learned how to take basic ideas and
solve really difficult problems that could

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:18.439
transfer that general skill into physics,
into computer science, into engineering, into

00:02:18.439 --> 00:02:20.359
economics, into philosophy.

00:02:20.359 --> 00:02:22.700
We're training kids to compete with computers.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:23.700
This is what computers do.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.370
They get it right every time.

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:29.319
And when we start training kids to compete
with those computers, we're setting them up

00:02:29.319 --> 00:02:30.319
for failure.

00:02:30.319 --> 00:02:37.900
And I think this is part of the problem is
this is the way that they are evaluated.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:39.730
So we need to bring these challenges earlier.

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:44.129
We need to put students in these situations
where they're not going to be able to get

00:02:44.129 --> 00:02:47.200
it right the first time or the second time
or the third time.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.019
And the kids know how to handle that.

00:02:49.019 --> 00:02:50.459
We trained it out of them.

00:02:50.459 --> 00:02:51.730
You look at kids when they're four.

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:54.950
I mean, a four year old kid can't do anything.

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:57.120
But they'll try.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:58.120
They'll fail.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:02:59.120
They'll cry.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:02.310
They'll run off to their room and then they'll
do something really amazing.

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:08.579
They'll come back really quickly and they'll
try again because they're four and they haven't

00:03:08.579 --> 00:03:11.549
learned yet how to quit forever.

00:03:11.549 --> 00:03:12.549
Learning's hard.

00:03:12.549 --> 00:03:13.549
It is painful.

00:03:13.549 --> 00:03:16.060
Yeah, it's a little frustrating, but you're
getting somewhere.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:17.060
That's.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:21.349
That's the sign that you are finally getting
somewhere with whatever you're trying to learn

00:03:21.349 --> 00:03:23.489
is when you're feeling this kind of discomfort.

00:03:23.489 --> 00:03:26.169
If you're comfortable, you're not learning
anything.

00:03:26.169 --> 00:03:31.909
I think one of the things that making this
transition to fighting through the problems

00:03:31.909 --> 00:03:36.689
as opposed to just knowing how to do it, when
I look at it, is understanding that it's a

00:03:36.689 --> 00:03:40.049
process and not an accumulation of knowledge.

00:03:40.049 --> 00:03:44.609
It's about being more playful, looking at
the problem and trying to learn about the

00:03:44.609 --> 00:03:49.029
situation, remove a few constraints from the
problem and explore or add constraints to

00:03:49.029 --> 00:03:51.420
the problem and explore a little bit.

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:57.359
What we don't do is show them how to do it
and then say, okay, this is how we do this

00:03:57.359 --> 00:03:58.359
problem.

00:03:58.359 --> 00:03:59.359
Here's another one that's similar.

00:03:59.359 --> 00:04:00.359
So we show them.

00:04:00.359 --> 00:04:01.359
We'll show them a problem.

00:04:01.359 --> 00:04:02.359
We'll work through it together.

00:04:02.359 --> 00:04:03.359
Like if they get completely stuck, we'll throw
them in some hands or throw them in, kind

00:04:03.359 --> 00:04:05.450
of guide them, guide them a little bit, and
then come back and summarize at the end.

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:06.450
Like that last step's pretty important.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:08.620
Is at the end is to pull out and say, Hey,
look at this general strategy we use.

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:11.139
You can motivate the steps towards that.

00:04:11.139 --> 00:04:13.730
And then they'll have another tool in their
tool kit.

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:16.470
I don't think of myself as teaching math.

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:20.730
I think of myself as teaching students problem-solving.

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:26.270
And by problem-solving, I mean teaching students
how to solve problems they've never seen before.

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:29.170
And math is a really powerful way to teach
that.

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:34.830
I think a lot of it is modeling for the student,
the questions we eventually want the student

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:39.930
to learn to ask themselves instead of saying,
Oh, you just add the seven and three.

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:40.930
Here's why.

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:45.081
What we do is walk them through the questions
that one might ask themselves in order to

00:04:45.081 --> 00:04:47.170
figure out how to do the thing.

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:50.470
We found that there were a lot of students
out there for whom textbooks were not sufficient.

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:51.470
They needed to interact with each other.

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:55.420
So we started with a community where we could
just bring the kids together online.

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:59.500
And then we started building online learning
systems, adaptive learning system, where you

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:03.680
just give the students problems and lead them
into harder and harder material.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:08.270
And I started developing videos to go along
with the adaptive learning system.

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:09.670
We have two online schools now.

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:10.670
We have learning centers around the country.

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:11.670
We have 12 learning centers right now.

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:15.160
We have an online community at APS online
that is, oh, hundreds of thousands of learners

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:16.870
from all over the world.

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:19.160
Our textbooks are comic books.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:24.050
A lot of students are visual learners, and
a lot of mathematical information can be conveyed

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:27.100
really effectively, visually.

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:29.230
We use it for pedagogical reasons as well.

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:35.590
You can't lecture a third grader, but in a
comic book you can simulate conversations.

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:38.080
We can model some of the strategies we want
them to use.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:41.080
We can model students asking questions.

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:43.840
We can model students working through challenges.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:49.410
We can model students being wrong and being
okay with that.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:54.830
All of these resources, they're readily accessible
to those who have the means to pay for them

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:58.140
and those who have parents who know to look
for them.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:01.870
Meanwhile, we're removing a lot of these opportunities
from schools.

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:06.680
The set of students are getting involved in
these programs is getting narrower and narrower.

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:13.000
So as a result, our country has a much narrower
pool of people who are going into advanced

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:14.710
math and science.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:20.250
The next step in our evolution is learning
how to work closely with schools where the

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:26.110
delivery mechanism there is the most important
person the student is working within their

00:06:26.110 --> 00:06:30.320
educational experience during the day, which
is their teacher.

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:35.050
So we're trying to learn how we are going
to learn how to work with teachers in the

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:40.760
classroom to empower them, to give their students
the experience we give our students.

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:46.780
So we're going to go and find that talent
ourselves by going into the schools so that

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:52.190
we can reach those students who don't have
the means or the knowledge to look for opportunities

00:06:52.190 --> 00:06:54.120
outside the classroom.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:07:00.780
We need to be drawing talent from across our
entire population.

